                <pb facs="0204" n="188" sameAs="frus:frus1951v07p1/pg188"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795B.00/2–2151: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Vincent</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bern</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-02-21T17:00:00+01:00">February 21,
                            1951—5 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1214. Swiss officials and local diplomats having occasion for contact with the
                    new Chinese Communist Minister and staff have informed me that the Chinese are
                    obdurate in their refusal to be brought into any kind of political discussion.
                    However, the Papal Nuncio today informed me that recently Zehnder (Swiss Under
                    Secretary of State) in conversation with Chinese Minister raised question of
                    Korea. The Minister surprisingly explained that Chinese Communist objectives in
                    Korea were political; that Communists never expected to achieve military
                    victory; and that they hope by prolonged engagement of American troops in Korea
                    situation will develop conducive to mutual compromise. This smacks of <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ex post facto</hi> rationalizing, or “sour grapes”
                    reasoning, but I report it for what it may be worth.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Vincent</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
